; The SQL bridge: a relational schema read as an olog, and back.
;
; It lives in tooling/ rather than core/, and the line is worth stating because
; the first cut got it wrong. `core/` is the LANGUAGE — the data model, the
; front end, and the standard library. Everything that bridges pol to something
; foreign lives here: `json` and `loadpath`, `lsp` and `mcp`. A PostgreSQL
; dialect parser is a bridge to a foreign NOTATION exactly as pol_lsp is a
; bridge to a foreign PROTOCOL, and putting one beside pol_data would assert
; that SQL is part of what pol IS. It is not; the engine cannot tell this
; library exists.
;
; Flat rather than lib/ + bin/, which is `json`'s and `loadpath`'s shape and not
; `lsp`'s: those two split because they produce their own executable. `pol sql`
; is a verb of the one CLI, so there is no second binary to separate from.
;
; It depends on [pol_data] and NOTHING ELSE — not the front end, not the
; interrogator. That is not an accident of what it happens to need: the import
; direction produces .pol TEXT (so it never builds a model, and a malformed DDL
; can never reach the engine), and the export direction reads a [Schema.t],
; which is a data-layer value. Keeping the dependency at one leaf is what makes
; "the mapping" a thing you can test without a filesystem or a parser.
;
; IO-free, like every library here; the `pol sql` verb in tooling/cli/ does the
; reading and writing.

(library
 (name pol_sql)
 (libraries pol_data))
